@Tutorial(time: 55) {
    @Intro(title: "OCL Expressions Deep Dive") {
        Master the Object Constraint Language (OCL) for expressing complex queries and constraints in ATL transformations.

        In this tutorial, you'll explore advanced OCL expressions including navigation, type operations, and sophisticated collection queries.

        You'll learn how to write precise, expressive transformations using OCL's powerful expression language.

        @Image(source: "atl-transformations.svg", alt: "ATL Transformations")
    }

    @Section(title: "Navigation and Type Operations") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            OCL provides operators for navigating model relationships and working with types. Navigation uses `.` for attributes and references, and `->` calls collection operations.

            Type operations like `oclIsTypeOf`, `oclIsKindOf`, and `oclAsType` enable runtime type checking and casting.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create a metamodel with inheritance.

                @Code(name: "Organisation.ecore", file: atl-05-step-01-organisation-metamodel.ecore)

                This metamodel demonstrates inheritance with Employee subtypes and department relationships.
            }

            @Step {
                Use navigation expressions.

                @Code(name: "OrganisationTransform.atl", file: atl-05-step-02-navigation.atl)

                Navigate through relationships using `.`, which also reaches a many-valued reference such as `self.employees`, and call collection operations such as `collect` and `size` with `->`.
            }

            @Step {
                Apply type checking operations.

                @Code(name: "OrganisationTransform.atl", file: atl-05-step-03-type-checking.atl)

                Use `oclIsTypeOf` for exact type matches and `oclIsKindOf` for subtype matches.
            }

            @Step {
                Perform type casting with `oclAsType`, checking the type first.

                @Code(name: "OrganisationTransform.atl", file: atl-05-step-04-type-casting.atl)

                `oclAsType(Organisation!Manager)` states that the employee is a Manager, so that `teamSize` can be read. The cast converts nothing: if the object is a Manager, or an instance of a subclass of Manager, it returns the same object. If it is not, swift-atl stops the transformation with a type error, and an undefined value stays undefined. The helper therefore checks with `oclIsKindOf`, which accepts exactly the objects that the cast accepts, and returns 0 for every other employee.
            }

            @Step {
                Create a sample department to try the cast on.

                @Code(name: "organisation.xmi", file: atl-05-step-05-sample-organisation.xmi)

                The department has two managers and one developer. The `xsi:type` attribute of each employee names its concrete class.
            }

            @Step {
                Write a query that reports the team size of every employee with the checked cast.

                @Code(name: "TeamSizes.atl", file: atl-05-step-06-guarded-query.atl)

                A query unit starts with `query Name = expression;` instead of a module header and may define helpers, so you can evaluate an expression over a model without a target metamodel. `Organisation!Employee.allInstances()` returns every employee of the model, managers and developers alike.
            }

            @Step {
                Evaluate the query with swift-atl.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: atl-05-step-07-run-guarded.sh)

                `swift-atl query` binds the model to the Organisation metamodel named by the `-- @path` directive and prints the value of the query. Ben Okafor is a Developer, so the check fails for him and the helper returns 0 without casting.
            }

            @Step {
                Write the same query without the check, casting every employee.

                @Code(name: "UncheckedTeamSizes.atl", file: atl-05-step-08-unchecked-query.atl)

                The cast is correct for the two managers, but not for the developer.
            }

            @Step {
                Evaluate the unchecked query.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: atl-05-step-09-run-unchecked.sh)

                The cast of Ben Okafor fails, and swift-atl reports the type error and exits with a non-zero status. Nothing else is printed: the error ends the whole query, not just the value for one employee. In a transformation, the same error ends the run before the target model is written. So an unchecked cast is only safe where the type is already certain, for example because the rule's source pattern or guard has established it.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Advanced OCL Operations") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            OCL provides sophisticated operations for strings, numbers, and complex collection queries. Understanding these operations enables precise transformation logic.

            Operations include string manipulation, numeric calculations, collection aggregation, and logical operations.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Use string operations.

                @Code(name: "OrganisationTransform.atl", file: atl-05-step-10-string-operations.atl)

                OCL string operations include `concat`, `substring`, `toUpper`, `toLower`, and `size`.
            }

            @Step {
                Apply numeric operations and comparisons.

                @Code(name: "OrganisationTransform.atl", file: atl-05-step-11-numeric-operations.atl)

                Numeric operations include arithmetic, comparison, and aggregate functions like `sum`, `min`, and `max`.
            }

            @Step {
                Combine complex collection operations.

                @Code(name: "OrganisationTransform.atl", file: atl-05-step-12-complex-collections.atl)

                Chain collection operations and use advanced queries like `flatten`, `union`, and `intersection`.
            }

            @Step {
                Build a complete transformation with advanced OCL.

                @Code(name: "OrganisationTransform.atl", file: atl-05-step-13-complete-transformation.atl)

                The complete transformation demonstrates sophisticated OCL expressions in a practical context.
            }
        }
    }

    @Assessments {
        @MultipleChoice {
            What is the difference between `.` and `->` in OCL navigation?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                `.` navigates features; `->` calls collection operations

                @Justification(reaction: "Correct!") {
                    Use `.` to navigate attributes and references, whether single-valued (e.g., `employee.department`) or many-valued (e.g., `department.employees`). Use `->` to call collection operations (e.g., `department.employees->collect(e | e.name)`).
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                `.` is faster than `->`

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite!") {
                    Performance isn't the distinguishing factor. They serve different purposes: navigating features and calling collection operations.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                `->` can only be used with helpers

                @Justification(reaction: "Incorrect!") {
                    `->` is used for collection operations anywhere in OCL expressions, not just with helpers.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            When should you use `oclIsKindOf` instead of `oclIsTypeOf`?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                When checking primitive types

                @Justification(reaction: "Try again!") {
                    Both operations work with class types. The difference is in how they handle inheritance.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                When you want to match subtypes as well as exact types

                @Justification(reaction: "That's right!") {
                    `oclIsKindOf` returns true for the exact type and all its subtypes, whilst `oclIsTypeOf` only matches the exact type.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                When you need better performance

                @Justification(reaction: "Not correct!") {
                    The choice between these operations is about semantics (subtypes vs exact types), not performance.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            Can you use `oclAsType` without checking the type first?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                No, ATL rejects a cast that no type check guards

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite!") {
                    The language does not require a check. `UncheckedTeamSizes.atl` casts without one and runs, and the cast of a manager succeeds. Only the cast of an object of another type fails.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Yes, and an object of the wrong type becomes undefined, so you can test the result with `oclIsUndefined()`

                @Justification(reaction: "Incorrect!") {
                    The cast does not return an undefined value for an object of the wrong type. Only an undefined receiver gives an undefined result. The cast of the developer stopped the query with a type error.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Yes, but if the object is not of that type, the cast stops the transformation with a type error

                @Justification(reaction: "Exactly!") {
                    `oclAsType` returns the object unchanged when it is of the type or a subtype, and otherwise reports "Cannot cast a value of type ..." and ends the run. Check with `oclIsKindOf` first unless the type is already certain.
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
